        "document_id": "P1",
        "block_id": "b0004",
        "quote": "Motivated by semi-parametric lan- guage models (LMs), which ground their decisions in external retrieved evidence, we use few-shot prompting to learn to condition LMs on information returned from the web using Google Search, a broad and constantly updated knowledge source."
      },
      {
        "document_id": "P1",
        "block_id": "b0010",
        "quote": "First, given a question we retrieve a set of relevant documents from the web using a search engine (§3.1). We then use the retrieved evidence to condition the LM through few-shot prompting (§3.2). Finally, we generate multiple candidate answers from each evidence and, to select the best answer, rerank them using scores computed using the same LM (§3.3)."
      },
      {
        "document_id": "P1",
        "block_id": "b0008",
        "quote": "In our work, we take a more light-weight approach without introducing learnable parameters. We push the limits of few-shot prompting and emphasize the need to establish strong baselines, aiming at gaining insights into the strengths and weaknesses of this generally applicable, due to its simplicity, approach."
      }
    ],
    "explanation": "The comment's tentative wording signals an impression, but its characterization of Google Search as the apparent main contribution is not well supported by the inspected paper text. The paper frames its approach around few-shot conditioning on web evidence and also describes candidate-answer generation and reranking; it presents Google Search as the retrieval source within that broader method. The excerpts support a mischaracterization, though they do not settle the review's broader judgments about novelty.",
